In your ASP.NET Core Web API, you can extract the user's email address from the Azure AD B2C access token by reading the claims in the HttpContext.User.


---

1. Extract Email from B2C Token in Controller

Azure AD B2C includes user details in the JWT token claims, including email (emails or preferred_username). Use HttpContext.User to retrieve it:

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using System.Linq;
using System.Security.Claims;

[Authorize] // Ensure the user is authenticated
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class UserController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("email")]
    public IActionResult GetUserEmail()
    {
        var emailClaim = User.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => 
            c.Type == "emails" || c.Type == ClaimTypes.Email || c.Type == "preferred_username");

        if (emailClaim != null)
        {
            return Ok(new { Email = emailClaim.Value });
        }

        return NotFound("Email claim not found in the token.");
    }
}


---

2. Ensure Your B2C Policy Includes Email Claims

To receive the email address in the token, check your User Flow / Custom Policy settings in Azure AD B2C:

Go to Azure AD B2C Portal

Open User Flows

Select the Sign-in or Sign-up policy

Under Application Claims, ensure email is selected


If using custom policies (Identity Experience Framework - IEF), ensure <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="email" /> is included in your TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml.


---

3. Decode the Token (For Debugging)

If the email is missing, check the token contents:

Decode the JWT at jwt.ms

Look for claims like emails, preferred_username, or upn


You can also log the token in your API:

var claims = User.Claims.Select(c => new { c.Type, c.Value });
return Ok(claims);

This helps identify the correct claim type.


---

4. Configure Authentication Middleware (If Not Already Done)

Ensure your JWT authentication middleware is correctly configured in Program.cs:

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection("AzureAdB2C"));

builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddControllers();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();


